-- Q2 [Amino Acid Structure & Stereochemistry] easy skill1 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Amino Acid Structure & Stereochemistry', 'A researcher completely hydrolyzes a globular protein purified from human liver tissue and determines the absolute configuration of each released \(\alpha\)-amino acid. Excluding glycine, which is achiral, the analysis is most likely to show that every chiral residue has which configuration?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "The D-configuration"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The L-configuration"}, {"label": "C", "text": "A racemic D/L mixture"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A meso configuration"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is B because every chiral amino acid found in naturally occurring proteins is in the L-configuration, related to L-glyceraldehyde. LibreTexts states that "All naturally occurring proteins from all living organisms consist of L-amino acids based on their structural similarities to L-glyceraldehyde" (5407-5408) and that "All naturally occurring amino acids in proteins are L, which corresponds to the S isomer, except cysteine" (5421); OpenStax adds that "In nature, the L-forms of amino acids are predominant in proteins" (3318). Because a protein is built entirely from one enantiomer, hydrolyzing it releases L-amino acids, not a mixture of stereoisomers. (Choice A) The D-configuration is the unnatural enantiomer; OpenStax notes that "Some D forms of amino acids are seen in the cell walls of bacteria" (3319) but not in normal proteins, so this would be wrong for a human liver protein. (Choice B) is correct: the residues hydrolyzed from a natural protein are L-amino acids. (Choice C) A racemic D/L mixture would require equal amounts of both enantiomers, but proteins are made from a single enantiomer (L), so no D-residues are present to create a mixture (5407-5408). (Choice D) A meso configuration requires a single molecule with multiple stereocenters and an internal mirror plane; a standard \(\alpha\)-amino acid has only one stereocenter and cannot be meso (5421). This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that proteinogenic amino acids in proteins adopt the L-configuration.', 'easy', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
