-- Q7 [Amino Acid Structure & Stereochemistry] hard skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Amino Acid Structure & Stereochemistry', 'A student notes that L-serine and L-cysteine share an identical backbone arrangement at the \(\alpha\)-carbon, with the \(-\text{COO}^-\), \(-\text{NH}_3^+\), and \(\text{H}\) groups oriented the same way, yet a reference table lists serine as (S) and cysteine as (R). The student is told that this descriptor difference does NOT reflect a real difference in spatial configuration. The contrast in (R)/(S) label is most directly explained by which feature of the cysteine side chain?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Cysteine is the D-enantiomer of serine"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Its sulfur outranks serine''s oxygen in priority"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Its \\(-\\text{SH}\\) group adds a second chiral center"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Serine''s \\(\\alpha\\)-carbon is not a chiral center"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is B because the (R)/(S) descriptor depends on Cahn-Ingold-Prelog priority ranking of the four groups on the \(\alpha\)-carbon, and the side-chain atom differs only in identity: serine carries a \(-\text{CH}_2\text{OH}\) group (a carbon bearing oxygen) while cysteine carries a \(-\text{CH}_2\text{SH}\) group (a carbon bearing sulfur). The textbook states that "L-cysteine is also (R)-cysteine" and that this "reflects the human decision to give a sulfur atom a higher priority than a carbon atom and does not reflect a real difference in configuration" (LibreTexts 5460-5461). Because sulfur outranks the groups present in serine, the priority order around cysteine''s \(\alpha\)-carbon is reordered, flipping the descriptor from (S) to (R) even though both amino acids remain L (LibreTexts 5421). (Choice A) is incorrect: both serine and cysteine are L-amino acids, not enantiomers of each other; the (R) label is a CIP artifact, since "all naturally occurring amino acids in proteins are L... except cysteine" refers only to the descriptor (LibreTexts 5421, 5460). (Choice B) is correct: the higher priority of sulfur over oxygen/carbon reorders the CIP ranking and switches the label without changing the spatial arrangement (LibreTexts 5460-5461). (Choice C) is incorrect: every amino acid except glycine has a single chiral center, and the sulfhydryl group is a simple \(-\text{SH}\), so cysteine has only one stereocenter, not two (LibreTexts 5399, 5263). (Choice D) is incorrect: serine''s \(\alpha\)-carbon bears four different groups and is chiral, since only glycine ("whose side chain is H") is achiral (LibreTexts 5399). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the CIP priority concept to explain why two structurally analogous L-amino acids receive different (R)/(S) labels.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
