-- Q10 [Side-Chain Classification] medium skill1 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Side-Chain Classification', 'A researcher sorts the 20 proteinogenic amino acids by the polarity of their side chains. Methionine, whose side chain terminates in a thioether \(\left(-\text{S}-\text{CH}_3\right)\), is being assigned to a class. Based on the chemical character of this side chain, into which class does methionine belong?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Polar uncharged, like cysteine"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Acidic, like glutamate"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Basic, like lysine"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Nonpolar (hydrophobic)"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is D because methionine is classified as a nonpolar, hydrophobic amino acid even though its side chain contains sulfur. LibreTexts states that "Methionine, one of the sulfur-containing amino acids, is usually classified as a nonpolar, hydrophobic amino acid. The terminal methyl group is a thioether, which generally cannot form a permanent dipole within the molecule and retains low solubility" (LibreTexts 5257-5258), and lists methionine among the nonpolar amino acids (LibreTexts 5183). OpenStax agrees that "valine, methionine, and alanine are nonpolar or hydrophobic in nature" (OpenStax 4278). Because the thioether sulfur cannot generate a permanent dipole, the mere presence of sulfur does not make methionine polar. (Choice A) is wrong: the polar uncharged class contains "the hydroxyl- and sulfhydryl-containing amino acids, serine, threonine, and cysteine" (LibreTexts 5263-5264); cysteine is the OTHER sulfur residue, but its sulfhydryl \(\left(-\text{SH}\right)\) can form a permanent dipole, so cysteine, not methionine, is polar uncharged. (Choice B) is wrong: the acidic amino acids are only "glutamic acid (glutamate), and aspartic acid (aspartate)" with carboxylic acid side chains (LibreTexts 5264-5265), which methionine lacks. (Choice C) is wrong: the basic amino acids are "lysine, arginine, and histidine," which "contain amine functional groups that can be protonated to carry a full charge" (LibreTexts 5265-5266); methionine''s side chain has no such amine. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the side-chain class to which methionine is assigned.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 100)
  RETURNING id
)
