-- Q11 [Side-Chain Classification] easy skill1 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Side-Chain Classification', 'A protein engineer is redesigning an active site and needs to introduce residues whose side chains can be protonated to carry a full positive charge at the appropriate pH. Which set of amino acids belongs to the basic class she should draw from?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Aspartate, glutamate, lysine"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Lysine, arginine, histidine"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Asparagine, glutamine, serine"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Arginine, lysine, tryptophan"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is B because the textbook explicitly groups lysine, arginine, and histidine together as the basic amino acids: their side chains contain amine functional groups (a primary amine on lysine, a guanidino group on arginine, and an imidazole ring on histidine) that can be protonated to carry a full positive charge. The engineer who wants residues capable of bearing a full positive charge must therefore draw from exactly this trio. (Choice A) is incorrect because aspartate and glutamate are the acidic amino acids, which carry carboxylic acid side chains capable of fully ionizing to a negative charge, not a positive one; only lysine in that set is actually basic. (Choice B) is correct: lysine, arginine, and histidine are the three basic amino acids whose amine/guanidino/imidazole side chains can be protonated to a full positive charge. (Choice C) is incorrect because asparagine and glutamine are amide-containing and serine is hydroxyl-containing; all three are polar uncharged residues that form dipoles but do not gain a full positive charge. (Choice D) is incorrect because, although arginine and lysine are basic, tryptophan is an aromatic residue whose indole nitrogen lone pair is tied up in ring resonance and is unavailable to accept a proton, so tryptophan is not basic. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the side-chain classification of the standard amino acids and identify the three that are basic.', 'easy', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 70)
  RETURNING id
)
