-- Q12 [Side-Chain Classification] medium skill2 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Side-Chain Classification', 'A student compiles the table below, listing each amino acid''s side-chain functional group and the polarity class she assigns to it. She is checking her work against the standard classification of the 20 proteinogenic amino acids.

| Amino acid | Side-chain functional group | Class assigned |
|---|---|---|
| Serine | hydroxyl \(-\text{CH}_2\text{OH}\) | nonpolar |
| Glutamate | carboxylate \(-\text{COO}^-\) | basic |
| Tyrosine | phenol (hydroxyl on an aromatic ring) | aromatic |
| Histidine | imidazole | polar uncharged |

Based on the standard classification scheme, the row whose class assignment is CORRECT is the one for which amino acid?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Serine"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Glutamate"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Tyrosine"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Histidine"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is C because the tyrosine side chain is a phenol — a hydroxyl group bonded directly to an aromatic ring — and tyrosine is grouped with phenylalanine and tryptophan as one of the three aromatic amino acids. The textbook states that ''the aromatic amino acids (phenylalanine, tyrosine, and tryptophan) contain an aromatic functional group,'' and notes that ''the hydroxyl group in tyrosine increases its reactivity and solubility compared to phenylalanine,'' confirming the phenol is on a ring system. Reading each row''s listed functional group and assigning the class shows that only the tyrosine row matches the standard scheme. (Choice A) Serine carries a hydroxyl side chain and is listed among the ''hydroxyl-...containing amino acids, serine, threonine, and cysteine'' within the polar uncharged class — it is polar uncharged, not nonpolar, so the serine row is mislabeled. (Choice B) Glutamate''s carboxylate side chain makes it acidic; the textbook says ''glutamic acid (glutamate), and aspartic acid (aspartate) constitute the acidic amino acids,'' so labeling it basic reverses its actual class. (Choice C) is correct: the phenol-bearing tyrosine is correctly placed in the aromatic class. (Choice D) Histidine is named among ''the basic amino acids, lysine, arginine, and histidine,'' so its imidazole side chain places it in the basic class, not polar uncharged. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the textbook''s classification rules to the structural feature listed in each table row to determine which amino acid is paired with the correct polarity class.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
