-- Q15 [Side-Chain Classification] medium skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Side-Chain Classification', 'A student compiles a reference table that lists four amino acids alongside the heteroatom-bearing functional feature of each R-group, intending to sort them by side-chain class. Based on the side-chain feature shown, which residue is correctly assigned to the polar-uncharged class?

| Residue | Side-chain heteroatom feature |
|---|---|
| Valine | none (hydrocarbon only) |
| Threonine | hydroxyl O \(-\text{OH}\) |
| Aspartate | carboxylate O \(-\text{COO}^-\) |
| Lysine | amino N \(-\text{NH}_3^+\) |', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Valine"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Threonine"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Aspartate"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Lysine"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is B because the polar-uncharged class is defined by side chains that carry a heteroatom (O, S, or N) capable of forming a permanent dipole without bearing a full charge, and the hydroxyl group of threonine fits this exactly. LibreTexts states that within the polar uncharged class "the side chains contain heteroatoms (O, S, or N) capable of forming permanent dipoles within the R-group. These include the hydroxyl- and sulfhydryl-containing amino acids, serine, threonine, and cysteine" (LibreTexts Biochemistry, lines 5262-5263). A threonine \(-\text{OH}\) provides this neutral polar dipole, placing it squarely in the polar-uncharged group rather than in any charged class. (Choice A) is incorrect: valine is one of the aliphatic amino acids that "typically contain straight or branched hydrocarbon chains" with no heteroatom, making it nonpolar/hydrophobic, not polar-uncharged (lines 5241-5242). (Choice B) is correct: threonine''s hydroxyl heteroatom makes it a polar-uncharged residue (lines 5262-5263). (Choice C) is incorrect: aspartate is polar, but its carboxylate side chain places it in the acidic class — "aspartic acid (aspartate) constitute the acidic amino acids and contain side chains with carboxylic acid functional groups capable of fully ionizing in solution" — so it carries a full negative charge rather than being uncharged (lines 5264-5266). (Choice D) is incorrect: lysine is one of "the basic amino acids... contain[ing] amine functional groups that can be protonated to carry a full charge," placing it in the basic class, not the polar-uncharged class (lines 5266-5267). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the heteroatom-and-charge definition of each side-chain class to the tabulated feature of each residue and select the one whose feature matches the polar-uncharged criterion.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
