-- Q18 [Hydrophobicity & Unique Residues] easy skill1 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Hydrophobicity & Unique Residues', 'A water-soluble enzyme folds into its native conformation in the cytosol. Within this folded structure, a valine residue''s nonpolar isopropyl side chain is most likely positioned in which location?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Exposed on the protein''s surface"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Spanning a lipid bilayer"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Covalently bonded to the backbone amide"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Buried in the protein''s interior"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is D because the nonpolar (hydrophobic) side chains of residues such as valine are excluded from water and pack into the protein''s interior when a soluble protein folds. LibreTexts states that "a continuous stretch of amino acids found to have a high average hydrophobicity (low hydrophilicity) is probably buried in the interior of a protein away from the aqueous environment" (LibreTexts 5367-5369), and shows the valine 143 side chain "surrounded by nonpolar amino acids" in a buried hydrophobic cluster (LibreTexts 4225-4226). OpenStax likewise classifies valine as "nonpolar or hydrophobic in nature" (OpenStax 4277-4278) and asks which categories sit in the interior versus the surface (OpenStax 4271-4272). (Choice A) is reversed: because hydrophobic stretches are buried away from the aqueous environment, the water-facing surface is where the polar and charged (hydrophilic) residues sit, not a nonpolar valine (LibreTexts 5367-5369; OpenStax 4271-4272). (Choice B) describes the hydrophobic transmembrane segment of an integral membrane protein, which "consists of 20-25 amino acids" and spans the bilayer (OpenStax 6648); this enzyme is water-soluble and cytosolic, not membrane-embedded, so its valine is not in a bilayer. (Choice C) misstates protein architecture: each amino acid''s "R group side chain [is] oriented away from the backbone" and is attached at the \(\alpha\)-carbon, not separately covalently bonded to the backbone amide (LibreTexts 3973-3974). (Choice D) is correct: a nonpolar valine side chain is sequestered from water in the hydrophobic core of the folded soluble protein (LibreTexts 5367-5369, 4225-4226). This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that hydrophobic residues partition into the protein interior away from water while hydrophilic residues sit on the surface.', 'easy', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
