-- Q19 [Hydrophobicity & Unique Residues] easy skill1 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Hydrophobicity & Unique Residues', 'A biochemist examining the structures of the 20 standard amino acids notes that one residue''s side chain loops back and bonds to the nitrogen of its own \(\alpha\)-amino group, leaving that nitrogen embedded in a five-membered ring. Which structural feature is this biochemist describing?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Proline''s side chain joined to the amino nitrogen"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Methionine''s sulfur-containing side chain"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Phenylalanine''s aromatic side-chain ring"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Aspartate''s carboxylate side chain"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is A because proline is the one standard amino acid whose hydrocarbon side chain has cyclized with the terminal amine, creating a unique five-membered, non-aromatic ring; its amino group is therefore not separate from the side chain, making the nitrogen part of the ring rather than a free primary amino group. The textbook states that for proline "the hydrocarbon side chain has cyclized with the terminal amine, creating a unique 5-membered ring, non-aromatic ring structure" (LibreTexts 5243-5247) and that proline "has an R group that is linked to the amino group, forming a ring-like structure" (OpenStax 4280-4282). (Choice A) is correct: only proline''s side chain bonds back onto its own \(\alpha\)-amino nitrogen to form the five-membered ring described. (Choice B) is wrong because the distinctive feature of methionine is that it is "one of the sulfur-containing amino acids" (LibreTexts 5257), a side-chain sulfur atom, not a ring closed onto the amino nitrogen. (Choice C) is wrong because phenylalanine''s ring is an "aromatic functional group" on its side chain (LibreTexts 5248-5249) that is separate from the backbone amino group, not fused to it. (Choice D) is wrong because aspartate is one of "the acidic amino acids" whose side chains contain "carboxylic acid functional groups" (LibreTexts 5264-5265), a carboxylate group rather than a ring involving the amino nitrogen. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the distinctive structural identity of proline among the standard amino acids.', 'easy', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
