-- Q22 [Hydrophobicity & Unique Residues] hard skill1 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Hydrophobicity & Unique Residues', 'A researcher characterizing distinctive side-chain features of individual amino acids drafts the following pairings. Consider which of the residue–feature pairings are correct.

I. Tryptophan carries a bicyclic indole ring that contains a nitrogen atom.
II. Tyrosine bears a phenol (a hydroxyl bonded directly to the aromatic ring) that raises its reactivity and solubility relative to phenylalanine.
III. Methionine presents a free thiol \(-\text{SH}\) side chain.', '[{"label": "A", "text": "III only"}, {"label": "B", "text": "I and III only"}, {"label": "C", "text": "II and III only"}, {"label": "D", "text": "I and II only"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is D because statements I and II describe genuine distinctive features while statement III misassigns a thiol to the wrong sulfur-containing residue. Tryptophan''s side chain is an indole ring whose nitrogen sits in a bicyclic aromatic system (LibreTexts 5277), and tyrosine''s phenol—a hydroxyl bonded directly to the aromatic ring—is exactly what increases its reactivity and solubility compared with phenylalanine (LibreTexts 5255–5256). Methionine''s sulfur is held in a thioether linkage that cannot form a permanent dipole and retains low solubility (LibreTexts 5257–5258); the free thiol \(-\text{SH}\) (sulfhydryl) belongs to cysteine instead (LibreTexts 5263), so statement III is false. The pairings that hold are therefore I and II only. (Choice A) III only selects the single false statement, treating methionine as if it carried cysteine''s thiol while discarding both true pairings. (Choice B) I and III only keeps the correct tryptophan pairing but again accepts the wrong-sulfur thiol for methionine. (Choice C) II and III only keeps the correct tyrosine pairing but still accepts the false methionine–thiol pairing while dropping the true tryptophan feature. (Choice D) is correct: I and II are the residue–feature pairings supported by the text, and III is rejected because methionine''s sulfur is a thioether, not a free thiol. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the specific side-chain structures of tryptophan, tyrosine, phenylalanine, methionine, and cysteine and apply them to judge each pairing.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
