-- Q23 [Hydrophobicity & Unique Residues] hard skill4 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Hydrophobicity & Unique Residues', 'A researcher analyzing a newly solved protein structure uses Kyte-Doolittle hydropathy values, where a continuous stretch of high average hydrophobicity is taken to lie buried in the protein interior, away from water. Two contiguous tripeptide stretches are flagged: Stretch 1 = Lys-Asp-Arg and Stretch 2 = Ile-Val-Leu.

| Residue | Kyte-Doolittle value |
|---------|----------------------|
| Ile | +4.5 |
| Val | +4.2 |
| Leu | +3.8 |
| Lys | -3.9 |
| Asp | -3.5 |
| Arg | -4.5 |

Based on the tabulated values, which stretch most likely lies in the buried hydrophobic core?

A. Stretch 1 (Lys-Asp-Arg), because it has more atoms per residue
B. Stretch 1 (Lys-Asp-Arg), because its values are far from zero
C. Stretch 2 (Ile-Val-Leu)
D. Neither stretch; both lie on the protein surface', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Stretch 1 (Lys-Asp-Arg), because it has more atoms per residue"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Stretch 1 (Lys-Asp-Arg), because its values are far from zero"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Stretch 2 (Ile-Val-Leu)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Neither stretch; both lie on the protein surface"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is C because the buried-core rule keys on the SIGN and average of the Kyte-Doolittle values (where + is hydrophobic), not on their absolute magnitude or residue count. LibreTexts states that "a continuous stretch of amino acids found to have a high average hydrophobicity (low hydrophilicity) is probably buried in the interior of a protein away from the aqueous environment" (libretexts-biochemistry.txt, lines 5367-5369), and clarifies the convention that on the Kyte-Doolittle scale "+ is hydrophobic" (line 5385). The Ile/Val/Leu stretch averages about +4.2 (Ile +4.5, Val +4.2, Leu +3.8 per the table at lines 5338, 5358, 5340), a high POSITIVE average that maps to the hydrophobic interior; the Lys/Asp/Arg stretch averages about -4.0 (Lys -3.9, Asp -3.5, Arg -4.5 at lines 5342, 5326, 5322), a high-hydrophilicity stretch that belongs at the water-exposed surface. (Choice A) is wrong because the rule maps topology from hydropathy values, not from how many atoms a residue has; counting atoms or residues is the wrong quantity to aggregate. (Choice B) is wrong because magnitude alone ("far from zero") ignores sign — Lys/Asp/Arg are far from zero but strongly NEGATIVE, which signals hydrophilicity and a surface location, the reverse of buried. (Choice C) is correct: averaging the table values gives a high positive (hydrophobic) score for Ile-Val-Leu, so by the cited rule this stretch most likely lies in the buried hydrophobic core. (Choice D) is wrong because the values are not similar — one stretch is strongly hydrophobic and one strongly hydrophilic — so they cannot both be surface; the hydrophobic stretch is predicted to be buried. This is a Data-based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must interpret tabulated Kyte-Doolittle values, aggregate them across a stretch, and map the averaged sign to protein topology.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 4, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
