-- Q24 [Hydrophobicity & Unique Residues] medium skill1 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Hydrophobicity & Unique Residues', 'A researcher is characterizing a protein and identifies one aromatic residue whose side chain carries a hydroxyl group bonded directly to the aromatic ring, giving that residue greater reactivity and aqueous solubility than its non-hydroxylated aromatic counterpart. Which amino acid does this residue correspond to?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Tyrosine"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Phenylalanine"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Tryptophan"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Serine"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is A because tyrosine is the aromatic amino acid that bears a phenolic hydroxyl group, meaning the \(-\text{OH}\) is attached directly to a carbon of its aromatic ring. LibreTexts notes that ''the aromatic amino acids (phenylalanine, tyrosine, and tryptophan) contain an aromatic functional group'' and that ''the hydroxyl group in tyrosine increases its reactivity and solubility compared to phenylalanine'' (LibreTexts lines 5248-5256). A phenol is specifically defined as the group formed when ''the hydroxyl group is directly attached to an aromatic ring'' (LibreTexts lines 1115, 1122), which is exactly the feature that distinguishes tyrosine. (Choice A) is correct: tyrosine''s ring-bound phenolic \(-\text{OH}\) is the unique feature that raises its reactivity and solubility relative to phenylalanine. (Choice B) Phenylalanine is aromatic but its side chain is an unsubstituted benzyl group with NO ring hydroxyl, so it is the less reactive, less soluble counterpart described, not the residue in question (LibreTexts lines 5255-5256). (Choice C) Tryptophan is aromatic but its side chain is an indole ring system that contains a nitrogen and no phenolic \(-\text{OH}\) (LibreTexts line 5277). (Choice D) Serine does carry an \(-\text{OH}\), but it is an aliphatic \(-\text{CH}_2\text{OH}\) on a non-aromatic side chain and serine is classed among the polar uncharged amino acids, not the aromatic ones (LibreTexts lines 5262-5263). This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall which aromatic amino acid is distinguished by a phenolic hydroxyl on its ring.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 100)
  RETURNING id
)
