-- Q25 [Hydrophobicity & Unique Residues] hard skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Hydrophobicity & Unique Residues', 'A researcher characterizing a side chain finds that it is built around a single aromatic ring, yet the ring is a plain benzene carrying no hydroxyl group and no nitrogen, making the residue among the most hydrophobic of the aromatic amino acids and giving it none of the added reactivity seen in its phenol-bearing relative. Which amino acid is being described?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Tyrosine"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Phenylalanine"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Tryptophan"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Leucine"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is B because phenylalanine is the aromatic amino acid whose side chain is an unsubstituted benzene ring — it carries no hydroxyl and no ring nitrogen. LibreTexts states that "the aromatic amino acids (phenylalanine, tyrosine, and tryptophan) contain an aromatic functional group, making them largely nonpolar and hydrophobic due to their high carbon-to-hydrogen ratio," and that "the hydroxyl group in tyrosine increases its reactivity and solubility compared to phenylalanine" (libretexts-biochemistry.txt lines 5248-5256); the hydrophobicity table lists phenylalanine (2.8) above tryptophan (-0.9) and tyrosine (-1.3), confirming it is the most hydrophobic of the aromatic trio (line 5346). With no -OH and no nitrogen on its ring, phenylalanine is the plainest and most hydrophobic of the aromatic trio, matching every clue in the stem. (Choice A) Tyrosine is aromatic, but its ring is a phenol — a hydroxyl directly bonded to the aromatic ring (lines 1115, 1122) — and that -OH "increases its reactivity and solubility compared to phenylalanine" (lines 5255-5256), which directly contradicts the "no hydroxyl" and "none of the added reactivity" clues. (Choice B) is correct: it is the unsubstituted-benzene aromatic residue lacking both the ring -OH and ring nitrogen (lines 5248-5256). (Choice C) Tryptophan is aromatic but its side chain is an indole ring that contains a nitrogen (lines 5276-5279), so it fails the "no nitrogen" condition. (Choice D) Leucine is hydrophobic, but it is an aliphatic, non-aromatic amino acid with a branched hydrocarbon side chain (lines 5241-5244), so it has no aromatic ring at all. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the structural rules for aromatic side chains — presence of a ring, a ring -OH, or a ring nitrogen — to negate three classes of residue and identify the one residue that satisfies all of the described constraints.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
